Snes9x (https://github.com/libretro/snes9x, the Snes9x licence, free use and never for sale) runs the Super Nintendo,
and like every libretro core it says nothing of the instructions it runs. The tools apply this change of Dreamports'
own to its source before building it on the contributor's computer (tools/consoles.mjs, tools/libretro/build.mjs), so
the program that plays the original (tools/libretro/host.c) can ask to be told of each one: its address, bank and all,
where that is in the cartridge's ROM whichever bank shows it, its first byte, and the processor's registers with the
flags M, X and E, which say how long each of its instructions is. That's how the code map learns which functions ran in
play and the mode each ran in (tools/snes/functions.mjs), and gameplay proven which instructions and which way each
branch went (tools/lib/played.mjs).

The processor only calls the function it was given, when it was given one, between reading an instruction and running
it: nothing of the console changes, so every frame's picture and sound are the same with it or without. Nothing of
Snes9x is in the repository but the lines of its own this change sits between.

